The proposed answer is incorrect. At Fort Donelson in February 1862, Generals John B. Floyd and Gideon Pillow actually fled the fort before the surrender, escaping across the Cumberland River with a portion of the troops (along with cavalry officer Nathan Bedford Forrest). Command then devolved upon Brigadier General **Simon Bolivar Buckner, Jr.**, who opened correspondence with Grant on February 16, 1862, requesting terms. Grant famously replied that no terms except "an unconditional and immediate surrender can be accepted," adding that Buckner should "propose terms at once." Buckner, an old friend of Grant's from the pre-war army, accepted these harsh terms and formally surrendered the garrison of roughly 12,000–15,000 men—the first major Union victory of the war.

Gideon Pillow did not surrender; he was criticized for abandoning his command and later was reassigned to lesser duties.

